; recordledger.eval - deleting a monster, a prop or a door in the editor is no
; one-pipeline violation (code-review C355). Judged by tools/EditorTest.py phase
; 32, on a scratch copy of dungeon-demo, under `pipelineguard strict`: a
; violation is an assert, and the run ends there.
;
; The damage ledger knows a watched hit-point value by its ADDRESS. The editor
; erases from the middle of the monster, prop and door lists while the world
; runs, so the next one along slid onto the freed address and was judged
; against the deleted one's baseline: a violation naming the deleted object,
; or under strict an abort. Each removal here is staged so the one that slides
; up has OTHER hit points than the one it replaces - the coward (10) before
; the skeleton (16) before the archer (12) before the warrior (22); a barrel
; (8) before a crate battered to 7 before one battered to 5; a door before a
; battered one before one battered further - and each is followed by a step,
; whose first checkpoint ("outside the world update") is where it was caught.
; Every way the editor removes one: the erase ladder (monster, prop, door),
; the inspectors' Delete (monster, prop, door) and a wall painted over a
; monster.

logecho on
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
seed 11
freeze on
pipelineguard on
pipelineguard strict on
editor
editor place decorations barrel 16 16
editor place decorations crate 18 16
editor place decorations crate 20 16
smash 18 16 3
smash 20 16 5
editor drag walls wall_marble 1 5 3 5 5 5 7 5 9 5 11 5
editor place doors wooden_door 2 5
editor place doors wooden_door 6 5
editor place doors wooden_door 10 5
smash 6 5 6
smash 10 5 12
step 1
echo --- staged ---
monsters
breakables
pipeline
editor erase 12 1
step 1
editor inspect 12 6
editor inspector delete
step 1
editor drag walls wall_marble 25 7
step 1
editor erase 16 16
step 1
editor inspect 18 16
editor inspector delete
step 1
editor erase 2 5
step 1
editor inspect 6 5
editor inspector delete
step 1
echo --- erased ---
monsters
breakables
pipeline
echo --- end ---
